  <main>
    <h1>MeowTrail Tips & Strategy</h1>

    <h2>Beginner Tips</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Start with 4-cells</strong> — A black cell with "4" means all 4 neighbors must have bulbs. Place them immediately.</li>
      <li><strong>Start with 0-cells</strong> — A "0" means none of its neighbors can have bulbs. Mark them as blocked.</li>
      <li><strong>Work from corners</strong> — Corner cells can only be lit from two directions, making them easier to reason about.</li>
      <li><strong>Mark X for impossible spots</strong> — If placing a bulb would violate a number constraint, mark that cell as empty.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Intermediate Techniques</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Line-of-sight elimination</strong> — If a bulb is placed, no other bulb can be in its row/column until a black cell. Eliminate those positions.</li>
      <li><strong>Counting remaining neighbors</strong> — If a "3" cell has 2 neighbors already blocked, the remaining 2 must both have bulbs.</li>
      <li><strong>Cross-hatching</strong> — Use the intersection of row and column constraints to narrow down bulb positions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Advanced Strategies</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Chain reasoning</strong> — Placing one bulb forces or eliminates others. Follow the chain until you reach a contradiction or solution.</li>
      <li><strong>Parity checks</strong> — Some grid sections have forced patterns based on the number of available cells vs. required bulbs.</li>
      <li><strong>Hypothetical reasoning</strong> — If placing a bulb at position X leads to a contradiction, X must be empty.</li>
    </ol>
  </main>
